              "children": [
                {
                  "id": "lede",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "If your trailers are on the road every day, staying compliant with DOT trailer regulations isn't just about avoiding fines. It's about protecting your drivers, reducing costly downtime, and keeping deliveries on schedule. Whether you manage an entire fleet or operate a handful of trailers, understanding DOT trailer inspection requirements helps you catch small issues before they become expensive repairs or roadside violations. Regular inspections and professional <a href=\"/service\">trailer service</a> also help extend the life of your equipment while giving you confidence that every trailer is ready for the miles ahead.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full",
                    "anchor": "post-lede"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "fig-1",
                  "type": "image",
                  "props": {
                    "width": "full",
                    "image": {
                      "src": "/img/blog/why-your-fleet-needs-to-follow-dot-trailer-regulations/1.jpg",
                      "width": 1280,
                      "height": 853,
                      "alt": "International trucks with hoods raised undergoing engine service in the Sun State shop"
                    }
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-what-are",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "What Are DOT Trailer Regulations?",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "what-are"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-what-are-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "DOT trailer regulations are federal safety standards established by the Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration (FMCSA). These rules are designed to ensure commercial trailers are safe to operate on public roads.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-what-are-2",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "<strong>The regulations cover everything from:</strong>",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "list-what-are-3",
                  "type": "prose-list",
                  "props": {
                    "ordered": false,
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "text": "Brake systems"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Tires and wheels"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Lighting and electrical systems"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Suspension components"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Coupling devices"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Frame condition"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Reflective tape and safety markings"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-what-are-4",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Drivers are also required to perform inspections before and after trips, while fleets must maintain maintenance records and repair identified safety defects promptly. According to the FMCSA, commercial motor vehicles must be systematically inspected, repaired, and maintained to help ensure safe operation. Following these requirements reduces the risk of equipment failures and helps improve roadway safety.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-inspection-req",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Understanding DOT Trailer Inspection Requirements",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "inspection-req"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-inspection-req-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Meeting DOT trailer inspection requirements involves more than a quick walk-around before leaving the yard. Routine inspections should verify that critical safety components are functioning properly before the trailer enters service.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-inspection-req-2",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "<strong>A typical inspection includes checking:</strong>",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
